and that was at Carmen Nita.

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:36.310
So, that's the schedules
you see where the first pass

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:38.810
that we put out a little over a week ago.

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:43.790
And, I hope that as we kind
of get our legs under us

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:46.000
that we can provide more
and more supports for y'all.

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:49.220
One thing we are very seriously
talking about are yes,

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:52.310
can we extend that schedule
to maybe think about

00:12:52.310 --> 00:12:54.060
what goals could be week by week

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:55.740
as we go through this crisis,

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:57.620
not just over the course of
the next couple of months

00:12:57.620 --> 00:12:58.860
during the traditional school year,

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:02.683
but to previous question
asker go through the summer,

00:13:02.683 --> 00:13:04.920
so that, you know, hey,
given your grade level,

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:06.900
given what you're trying to achieve,

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:09.320
what are reasonable
goals for you to achieve?

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:11.910
And it would be cool to
even switch it up like,

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:13.800
hey, here's the PE of the day.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:14.850
So what we're trying to do,

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:17.184
we have a small but mighty team,

00:13:17.184 --> 00:13:20.490
and we're gonna try to put
as much together as possible,

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:22.380
but that's a really cool
idea to think about.

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:23.970
How could we switch it
up day by day a bit?

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:26.390
Maybe we give a weekly schedule
or something like that.

00:13:26.390 --> 00:13:28.303
So, we're gonna work on that.

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:31.693
Let's see.

00:13:31.693 --> 00:13:34.050
There's other questions.

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:36.270
Some Facebook Tamra Adams says,

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:39.148
is this the new normal
of distance learning?

00:13:39.148 --> 00:13:43.670
How do you believe is the
best platform for assessments?

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:44.660
So Tamra, you know,

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:47.089
what you're asking is a
really intriguing question.

00:13:47.089 --> 00:13:49.940
I've been writing actually
just there was an op ed

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:51.960
that came out today that I wrote about,

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:53.636
you know, are a lot of people asking me,

00:13:53.636 --> 00:13:55.950
what's the new normal,
what's structurally,

00:13:55.950 --> 00:13:58.440
how is this crisis going
to fundamentally alter

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:01.020
what we do going forward and schooling

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:03.950
and obviously in a bunch of other ways?

00:14:03.950 --> 00:14:06.250
The way I've been thinking about it is,

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:07.700
given that this is, you know,

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:09.800
we're likely to go through
the end of the school year

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:13.260
and then summer, and then as
we go into next school year,

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:14.820
hopefully schools are open,

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:18.440
but then they might have to
close with fairly short notice

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:21.880
if the virus picks up again
in November, December.

00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:25.460
I think this is where the
hybrid of in-person learning

00:14:25.460 --> 00:14:28.410
and having a great online
tools that are accessible

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:31.570
and free it's starting to
become even more important.

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:34.190
Even before the crisis
we've talked a lot about

00:14:34.190 --> 00:14:36.776
that everyone learns at different paces

00:14:36.776 --> 00:14:40.570
and has gaps in their knowledge
if they're pushed together

00:14:40.570 --> 00:14:41.570
at a fixed pace.

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:43.190
Teachers have always known this.

00:14:43.190 --> 00:14:45.737
They have students who come
into a class on day one.

00:14:45.737 --> 00:14:47.900
There's some kids who
are ready to move ahead.

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:49.750
There's some kids who have gaps from two,

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:51.250
three grade levels behind.

00:14:51.250 --> 00:14:53.510
Teachers try to kinda teach to the middle

00:14:53.510 --> 00:14:55.090
but that leaves some kids a little bored,

00:14:55.090 --> 00:14:58.270
some kids a little lost, and
there was no way for them,

00:14:58.270 --> 00:14:59.610
you know, they're taught in ed schools

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:01.390
to try to differentiate
but it's really hard

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:04.560
if you're one teacher
in a classroom of 25, 30

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:06.700
or in some cases, 35 or 40 students.

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:08.360
And so even before the crisis,

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:11.720
all of us at Khan Academy have
been working with teachers

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:12.620
and districts to say,

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:15.090
okay, how can we support
teachers in that way

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:16.060
so they have the tools?

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:18.320
How can we be the teaching
assistant for that teacher?

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:20.870
So they have the tools to
give that personalization

00:15:20.870 --> 00:15:22.790
for every student so
they can fill their gaps

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:26.040
and if necessary, learn
along the class if necessary

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:27.790
and even accelerate as necessary.

00:15:27.790 --> 00:15:29.110
And then how do we do it with districts?

00:15:29.110 --> 00:15:31.430
So the districts can have a read on

00:15:31.430 --> 00:15:33.420
where their kids need help or not.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:36.080
And now all of a sudden when
you have these school closures,

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:38.110
the districts that have adopted that,

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:40.510
those types of models
where they are using tools

00:15:40.510 --> 00:15:43.450
like Khan Academy for even
20% of their class time

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:45.160
really is a strategic supplement.

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:47.460
It's been much easier
for them to transition

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:50.750
to the school closure world
because they can now lead,

00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:53.960
you know, they've already
rostered their kids, set them up,

00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:55.980
the students are used
to using these tools,

00:15:55.980 --> 00:15:57.920
the teachers are used to
reading the dashboards

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:01.140
and so they've been able to
lean quite heavily on these,

00:16:01.140 --> 00:16:04.950
say the teacher or the district
version of Khan Academy

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:06.800
and have a seamless transition.

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:11.800
And so I think, one of the
new normals is the emphasis

00:16:12.250 --> 00:16:15.230
on being able to blend is
going to be really important,

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:17.310
blend so that you can accelerate outcomes

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:18.630
during a normal school year,

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:21.330
but also blend so that
if for whatever reason

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:24.950
there's a school stoppage
that kids can keep learning

00:16:24.950 --> 00:16:27.570
and then the district and the
teachers can keep monitoring.

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:28.820
I think there's a whole other layer

00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:30.780
that's going to be really
interesting around,

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:33.190
you know, I've been talking to
some school districts lately,

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:34.410
they're doing all sorts of training

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:39.410
to help teachers start teaching
on Google Me or on Zoom.

00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:41.710
So I think that's gonna be a new muscle

00:16:41.710 --> 00:16:44.180
that even when schools are in session,

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:46.540
I think might actually be an added muscle

00:16:46.540 --> 00:16:49.640
that'll be really powerful for
a lot of types of schooling.

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:52.640
So, we will see, but a
lot of things going on.

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:54.240
I think one of the
biggest question questions

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:56.109
that everyone's thinking about is access.

00:16:56.109 --> 00:16:57.930
Obviously if you're watching this,

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:00.000
you have internet access at home,

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.780
but there's still a large
fraction of kids in the US

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:04.910
and even a larger fraction
globally who don't.

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:06.360
And I think this crisis shows us

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:09.790
that at home internet access
is not just a nice to have,

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:12.250
it's a must have, not
just to access things

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:14.000
like Khan Academy and Keep Learning,

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:16.740
but also to just be socially connected

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:18.480
and for your mental health when all of us

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:20.217
are having to be socially distanced.

00:17:20.217 --> 00:17:21.770
Great question though,

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:23.820
and something we're
thinking about it, a lot.

00:17:23.820 --> 00:17:26.410
- So Sal we have a question on Facebook

00:17:26.410 --> 00:17:31.090
from Elizabeth Warner, and
this is for a lot of folks.

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:32.740
So where can a parent find out more

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:34.633
about the upcoming parent
webinars next week?

00:17:34.633 --> 00:17:37.280
Where's the best place to
find these opportunities

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:38.390
to send parents to?

00:17:38.390 --> 00:17:40.150
And really what's the best place to find

00:17:40.150 --> 00:17:42.800
all of our resources that
we're developing right now?

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:46.610
- I could answer it, but I
don't wanna steal Dan's thunder

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:50.073
'cause he's actually the
guy (laughs ) coordinating.

00:17:51.518 --> 00:17:53.160
(speaks indistinctly)
why don't you tell us?

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:54.315
- Yeah, sure Sal.

00:17:54.315 --> 00:17:56.270
If you just go to
khanacademy.org right now,

00:17:56.270 --> 00:17:57.640
we have a blue ribbon at the top

00:17:57.640 --> 00:18:00.208
and it contains links
to all of our resources.

00:18:00.208 --> 00:18:02.710
Our parent webinars, the
ones that are upcoming

00:18:02.710 --> 00:18:05.240
are listed there as well as webinars

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:06.440
that we've already performed

00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:08.810
where you create links
there and it's available

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:12.163
for you to view so in case you
can't make any of them live,

00:18:12.163 --> 00:18:14.703
they are available to you
after the fact as well.

00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:16.573
- Yeah, exactly.

00:18:16.573 --> 00:18:19.090
You just go to our homepage
or actually any page

00:18:19.090 --> 00:18:20.680
on Khan Academy, that banner at the top

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:22.160
there's three or four links.

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:24.610
One of them is kind of, you know,

00:18:24.610 --> 00:18:27.440
we've been posting stuff
on the daily schedules

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:28.770
'cause a lot of people
have been looking at that

00:18:28.770 --> 00:18:30.710
and also that a remote
learning resources link

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:33.990
that you can see right now, and we are,

00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:35.760
and Dan is one of the many people

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:38.110
who is constantly updating
that to keep you informed

00:18:38.110 --> 00:18:40.890
and also stay tuned on social
media, our Facebook page,

00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:42.350
our Twitter, et cetera,

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:44.210
that's also where we're going
to be making announcements

00:18:44.210 --> 00:18:46.703
on new resources that we are adding.

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:50.330
All right.

00:18:50.330 --> 00:18:52.610
So there's other questions.

00:18:52.610 --> 00:18:54.760
So Facebook, from Facebook,

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:56.030
Mary Miller's asking any thoughts

00:18:56.030 --> 00:18:58.374
on adding more arts
courses to the curriculum?

00:18:58.374 --> 00:19:01.020
So, we don't have any major plans

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:02.890
to add significantly new ones.

00:19:02.890 --> 00:19:04.930
And then I don't think if
there's a little hum from yours

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:06.320
or maybe lower the microphone down.

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:07.430
I'm getting a hum.

00:19:07.430 --> 00:19:08.670
It's not horrible.

00:19:08.670 --> 00:19:10.030
Oh yes but (speaks indistinctly).

00:19:12.638 --> 00:19:13.750
(laughs)

00:19:13.750 --> 00:19:16.253
No, but on the art courses,

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:20.010
we aren't going to add
any new significant ones,

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:22.170
but we have a partnership
with Beth and Steven

00:19:22.170 --> 00:19:25.550
who do smart history, who've
been adding art history content

00:19:25.550 --> 00:19:27.770
on Khan Academy for many, many years now.

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:29.700
And so, if you haven't seen it already,

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:32.524
I do encourage you to explore
art history on Khan Academy.

00:19:32.524 --> 00:19:34.560
It's really, really interesting stuff

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:36.270
where they're having
these cool conversations

00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:39.020
about some of the most important
pieces of art in the world

00:19:39.020 --> 00:19:41.450
or actually almost all of
the important pieces of art

00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:42.300
in the world.

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:44.610
So encourage you to check that out.

00:19:44.610 --> 00:19:45.443
All right.

00:19:45.443 --> 00:19:50.443
So there's other questions
here from Facebook.

00:19:50.790 --> 00:19:53.690
Actually, let's see, there's
other questions below the.

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:56.070
So from YouTube, Kimberly Klein asks,

00:19:56.070 --> 00:20:00.690
what advice do you have for
GED students using the website?

00:20:00.690 --> 00:20:04.720
So Kimberly, my recommendation
to you as a GED student,

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:06.300
I think there's two things
you could do actually,

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:07.970
even though it's not built for GED,

00:20:07.970 --> 00:20:11.069
I think our SAT practice
could be great for the GED.

00:20:11.069 --> 00:20:13.580
It's covers roughly the same,

00:20:13.580 --> 00:20:15.250
you know, math reading and writing,

00:20:15.250 --> 00:20:16.970
and so if you get good at that,

00:20:16.970 --> 00:20:20.080
I'm confident that you will
do just fine on the GED.

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:22.710
If you feel like you have
some more foundational gaps,

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:24.680
especially in math or reading,

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:28.290
then I encourage you to try
to find your learning edge

00:20:28.290 --> 00:20:31.170
on Khan Academy math and
the Khan Academy English

00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:32.260
and language arts.

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:34.650
And you could do that by
just sampling and say,

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:37.610
okay, the algebra stuff
seems a little hard.

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:39.460
Okay, the seventh grade stuff
feels a little bit easy,

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:41.500
eighth grade feels right for me,

00:20:41.500 --> 00:20:43.350
and then you could take
the course challenge

00:20:43.350 --> 00:20:45.880
and use that to assess what
you know and don't know

00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:47.230
and then focus on the units

00:20:49.690 --> 00:20:51.300
where you might have larger gaps.

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:53.620
There's another way where
you can actually start

00:20:53.620 --> 00:20:58.000
at the earliest possible
stage at second grade

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:01.610
in English and language arts
and a second grade in math

00:21:01.610 --> 00:21:03.780
or even earlier in math you could start.

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:04.980
And just go from there

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:06.590
and if you feel like
it's ridiculously easy,

00:21:06.590 --> 00:21:08.500
you obviously can skip to the next grade.

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:09.340
But that actually might be,

00:21:09.340 --> 00:21:11.230
and you could take the
course challenges in math.

00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:13.330
That might be one of the most robust ways

00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:16.310
of making sure that you don't
have any debilitating gaps.

00:21:16.310 --> 00:21:19.840
What we've seen even a strong
students, A, B student,

00:21:19.840 --> 00:21:21.120
from middle school, high school,

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:23.910
even in third or fourth grade, you know,

00:21:23.910 --> 00:21:26.150
they might have a mastery of 99% of it

00:21:26.150 --> 00:21:28.363
but there's always that one like, oh,

00:21:28.363 --> 00:21:32.742
that's why the distributive
property works this way.

00:21:32.742 --> 00:21:35.110
Or things like that, things
that they've just, you know,

00:21:35.110 --> 00:21:37.610
taken for granted but even
that helps fill in the gap.

00:21:37.610 --> 00:21:40.551
So, I think that's
something that you can do.

00:21:40.551 --> 00:21:44.740
So, from Facebook Myuran Alara asks,

00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:47.490
how can we make sure that we
maintain a good mental health

00:21:47.490 --> 00:21:49.110
during these uncertain times?

00:21:49.110 --> 00:21:50.640
You are my hero.

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:52.023
Well, thank you Myuran.

00:21:53.169 --> 00:21:55.593
You're being very generous.

00:21:56.860 --> 00:22:01.340
On the mental health
side of things, you know,

00:22:01.340 --> 00:22:02.980
and this is something I think a lot about

00:22:02.980 --> 00:22:05.780
'cause even before this crisis, you know,

00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:08.280
sometimes life can get stressful,
jobs can get stressful,

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:11.630
school can get stressful,
what I have found invaluable

00:22:11.630 --> 00:22:13.150
and y'all have seen me
talk about this before

00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:14.820
has been meditation.

00:22:14.820 --> 00:22:17.860
And you know, meditation isn't
some fancy new age thing.

00:22:17.860 --> 00:22:19.680
I mean it is a fancy
new age thing as well.

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:20.513
(laughs)

00:22:20.513 --> 00:22:21.959
But it doesn't have to
be a fancy new age thing.

00:22:21.959 --> 00:22:24.771
You know, meditation, it literally can be,

00:22:24.771 --> 00:22:27.700
you know, you can start
five minutes in the morning

00:22:27.700 --> 00:22:29.430
and I've personally found the morning

00:22:29.430 --> 00:22:30.500
or right before I go to bed,

00:22:30.500 --> 00:22:32.760
but especially the morning it's about,

00:22:32.760 --> 00:22:34.740
is the best meetup when
the house is quiet,

00:22:34.740 --> 00:22:36.320
is the best time to meditate

00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:38.656
and it's literally just
finding a quiet space,

00:22:38.656 --> 00:22:40.950
sitting, you know, posture matters a lot.

00:22:40.950 --> 00:22:44.800
Sit up right, close
your eyes, be attentive

00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:46.600
and just try to observe your thoughts,

00:22:46.600 --> 00:22:49.120
and you'll realize that
you are not your thoughts.

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:51.760
That there's all this stuff
that's going on your thoughts.

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:52.860
I've got to get that thing done.

00:22:52.860 --> 00:22:54.180
Why did that person say that?

00:22:54.180 --> 00:22:55.770
And how am I going to do this?

00:22:55.770 --> 00:22:58.990
And, you know, what's
going on on that TV show

00:22:58.990 --> 00:23:01.360
that I'm watching and, you know, any,

00:23:01.360 --> 00:23:03.000
I know like what's gonna
happen with COVID rise?

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:04.640
You know, all of these
thoughts are going in our head

00:23:04.640 --> 00:23:07.500
right now, and what you
need to do is just learn

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:08.770
to observe those thoughts,

00:23:08.770 --> 00:23:11.000
and separate it yourself
from those thoughts.

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:13.160
And, you know, the a metaphor,

00:23:13.160 --> 00:23:14.930
we've done some guided
meditations on kind of going.

00:23:14.930 --> 00:23:17.420
There you have it fully
fans a put the screen.

00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:19.230
So you can go use these guided meditations

00:23:19.230 --> 00:23:20.790
if you want a little bit of health,

00:23:20.790 --> 00:23:23.070
but over time you'll be
able to do it on your own

00:23:23.070 --> 00:23:24.210
and you're gonna realize

00:23:24.210 --> 00:23:26.340
that as you rise above
the thoughts, you know,

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:28.800
if you do the thoughts
as cars on a highway,

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:30.100
you just kind of get out of the highway

00:23:30.100 --> 00:23:31.750
and just watch the cars go by,

00:23:31.750 --> 00:23:33.430
that the car starts slowing down.

00:23:33.430 --> 00:23:35.661
And you're gonna have these
moments where you're like,

00:23:35.661 --> 00:23:37.340
well, you're not even thinking about it,

00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:38.316
you're just not thinking.

00:23:38.316 --> 00:23:41.563
And when you have even
five minutes of that a day,

00:23:41.563 --> 00:23:44.260
and your mind will wander
and don't beat up yourself,

00:23:44.260 --> 00:23:46.420
just say, okay mind, that's
funny, you wandering,

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:47.470
come on back in.

00:23:47.470 --> 00:23:50.150
You're kind of like a sheep
herding dog for your mind

00:23:50.150 --> 00:23:51.980
and your mind are the sheeps (laughs)

00:23:51.980 --> 00:23:53.750
or you're just kind of,
that's a little sheep

00:23:53.750 --> 00:23:56.150
that's going astray and
you gotta bring it back.

00:23:56.150 --> 00:23:57.980
But I found if even five minutes a day,

00:23:57.980 --> 00:23:59.340
but over time you're going to like it,

00:23:59.340 --> 00:24:00.720
you're gonna wanna do
10 minutes, 15 minutes.

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:02.670
I do half an hour every morning.

00:24:02.670 --> 00:24:04.090
You're gonna have moments
in that half an hour

00:24:04.090 --> 00:24:05.590
where your mind is wandering but moments

00:24:05.590 --> 00:24:07.810
in that half an hour where
you're just completely still

00:24:07.810 --> 00:24:10.770
and you'll feel really happy
and really, really calm.

00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:13.610
And I found that it's not
just for that 30 minutes

00:24:13.610 --> 00:24:15.310
or that 10 minutes that you're meditating,

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:17.480
it just maybe makes you
more mentally resilient

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:18.313
through the day.

00:24:18.313 --> 00:24:22.200
It's harder for people to
trigger you, so to speak,

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:23.720
you get less worked up about things

00:24:23.720 --> 00:24:25.540
and you get less stressed about things

00:24:25.540 --> 00:24:29.290
and so that's my best advice and you know,

00:24:29.290 --> 00:24:30.850
just don't beat up on yourself,

00:24:30.850 --> 00:24:32.970
take everything one day
at a time, you know,

00:24:32.970 --> 00:24:37.057
to quote a frozen one and frozen
two, frozen one, you know,

00:24:37.057 --> 00:24:41.630
let it go and frozen two
do the next right thing

00:24:41.630 --> 00:24:43.920
which is a awfully deep philosophy

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:45.260
which is actually consistent

00:24:45.260 --> 00:24:47.758
with most of the mystical
traditions of most religions.

00:24:47.758 --> 00:24:49.837
(laughs)

00:24:49.837 --> 00:24:52.010
- So Sal unfortunately with the crisis

00:24:52.010 --> 00:24:53.350
affecting everyone around the world,

00:24:53.350 --> 00:24:55.400
this question is particularly important.

00:24:55.400 --> 00:24:57.877
So from YouTube online
education, Nepal says,

00:24:57.877 --> 00:25:01.110
hello Sal, I'm Pushpa from Nepal.

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:03.460
How can you move this
into different languages?

00:25:04.670 --> 00:25:05.771
- Great question Pashpa.

00:25:05.771 --> 00:25:09.220
So there are 40 translation projects

00:25:09.220 --> 00:25:11.240
of Khan Academy around the world,

00:25:11.240 --> 00:25:13.910
and there are different
degrees of fidelity.

00:25:13.910 --> 00:25:17.550
We have the richest
versions in Spanish-speaking

00:25:17.550 --> 00:25:19.450
Latin America, Brazilian, Portuguese,

00:25:19.450 --> 00:25:21.698
and we have actually a team in India

00:25:21.698 --> 00:25:26.080
that is trying to work on the
various languages in India

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:28.380
and aligned to the standards
in all of these places.

00:25:28.380 --> 00:25:29.213
And then of course you have

00:25:29.213 --> 00:25:31.360
this other 30 something projects going on.

00:25:31.360 --> 00:25:33.050
So that's what's going on right now.

00:25:33.050 --> 00:25:35.760
I'm actually not expert
on the state of Nepalis,

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:37.760
if that's of a particular interest,

00:25:37.760 --> 00:25:40.640
but if we don't already have
a translation project there,

00:25:40.640 --> 00:25:44.030
obviously we would love
people to start one.

00:25:44.030 --> 00:25:48.247
So that's one layer that
we think we can help with.

00:25:48.247 --> 00:25:50.560
And then the other layer is
in many parts of the world,

00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:52.560
things like internet access, et cetera.

00:25:52.560 --> 00:25:54.660
And, you know, there where we are try,

00:25:54.660 --> 00:25:57.390
we do try to work with
philanthropists, corporations,

00:25:57.390 --> 00:26:00.178
telecom carriers to
make it more accessible.

00:26:00.178 --> 00:26:02.853
But that's our best answer right now.

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:07.650
All right, YouTube, Samayam Hyundai

00:26:07.650 --> 00:26:10.230
is asking a really simple question.

00:26:10.230 --> 00:26:13.393
What do you think is the purpose of life?

00:26:15.510 --> 00:26:16.753
That's a good question.

00:26:16.753 --> 00:26:17.758
(laughs)

00:26:17.758 --> 00:26:20.700
Samayam, yeah, no I mean, you know,

00:26:20.700 --> 00:26:25.293
I think not to get to a
metaphysical or too deep,

00:26:26.830 --> 00:26:29.610
we find ourselves in this
big mystery and I think,

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:31.970
you know, current world events, you know,

00:26:31.970 --> 00:26:34.330
which feel almost surreal, dystopian,

00:26:34.330 --> 00:26:35.610
whatever you want to call it,

00:26:35.610 --> 00:26:39.070
I think really emphasize
'cause we're finding ourselves

00:26:39.070 --> 00:26:40.810
in this big mystery.

00:26:40.810 --> 00:26:45.810
And I think one of the purposes
is to reflect on the mystery

00:26:45.940 --> 00:26:50.240
and to experience this
mystery that we call life

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:51.560
in the universe and everything

00:26:51.560 --> 00:26:55.567
and obviously I'm making a few
references to Douglas Adams.

00:26:55.567 --> 00:26:58.200
But yeah, you know, I can't claim

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:00.170
that I know the purpose of life.

00:27:00.170 --> 00:27:02.260
I will say that sometimes in your moments

00:27:02.260 --> 00:27:03.670
when you can actually steal your thoughts

00:27:03.670 --> 00:27:06.400
and bring your mind away from,

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:08.220
I guess you could call it material reality

00:27:08.220 --> 00:27:10.900
and all of the distractions
and material reality,

00:27:10.900 --> 00:27:14.310
sometimes you can feel a
deep peace and connectedness

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:17.370
to the universe and whatever
you might want to call that.

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:19.970
And I think that actually
can sometimes make you feel

00:27:19.970 --> 00:27:23.190
a very grounded and very happy.

00:27:23.190 --> 00:27:26.400
And so, you know, I can't
necessarily describe

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:27.610
that as a purpose of life

00:27:27.610 --> 00:27:30.750
but I can give you maybe a lens on life.

00:27:30.750 --> 00:27:33.800
But it's a really, really good question.

00:27:33.800 --> 00:27:38.540
One met a one framework, you know,

00:27:38.540 --> 00:27:40.940
who knows if this is true is, you know,

00:27:40.940 --> 00:27:42.937
this is something of a
learning experience for you

00:27:42.937 --> 00:27:45.860
and obviously we talk about
learning at the academic level,

00:27:45.860 --> 00:27:48.920
but life itself is a
mega learning experience.

00:27:48.920 --> 00:27:52.860
And, you know, there's
frameworks of imagining

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:55.940
that every person on the
planet is another version

00:27:55.940 --> 00:27:58.070
of yourself and so you are, you know,

00:27:58.070 --> 00:27:59.180
we're all connected in some way.

00:27:59.180 --> 00:28:00.013
Who knows?

00:28:00.013 --> 00:28:01.430
I don't wanna get to a metaphysical

00:28:01.430 --> 00:28:03.730
'cause I don't know the answers,
but it's a deep question

00:28:03.730 --> 00:28:07.710
that I'm sure we will
(laughing) keep struggling with.

00:28:07.710 --> 00:28:11.440
Okay, so YouTube Gurpreet
is asking, how to deal

00:28:11.440 --> 00:28:14.783
with demotivation and how
to approach a new topic.

00:28:14.783 --> 00:28:18.280
I think number one thing with demotivation

00:28:18.280 --> 00:28:20.430
or procrastination is to just get started

00:28:20.430 --> 00:28:22.180
and I've talked about
it in other sessions.

00:28:22.180 --> 00:28:24.140
There's something called
the Pomodoro technique,

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:25.670
where you can kind of set a timer and say,

00:28:25.670 --> 00:28:27.500
I'm gonna do 20 minutes
and I'm gonna focus

00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:29.042
and I'm gonna get something done

00:28:29.042 --> 00:28:31.560
and then give yourself a break after that,

00:28:31.560 --> 00:28:34.210
force a break after that,
and then jump back in.

00:28:34.210 --> 00:28:37.198
And, you know, one thing that
I've always reminded myself

00:28:37.198 --> 00:28:39.636
is to just keep on moving.

00:28:39.636 --> 00:28:41.970
I actually am a big
believer in starting the day

00:28:41.970 --> 00:28:44.040
with making your bed and I
have a little bit of a ritual

00:28:44.040 --> 00:28:45.600
which includes meditation.

00:28:45.600 --> 00:28:47.312
I go do some pull-ups in my garage,

00:28:47.312 --> 00:28:52.312
I do some pushups, and the
reason why I do those things,

00:28:52.740 --> 00:28:54.299
it's not so much even for my health

00:28:54.299 --> 00:28:56.920
or to make my bed look nice,

00:28:56.920 --> 00:28:58.820
it gives me some wins early in the day.

00:28:58.820 --> 00:29:00.470
And I find that on days

00:29:00.470 --> 00:29:03.240
that I'm able to have some accomplishments

00:29:03.240 --> 00:29:05.570
within 20, 30 minutes of waking up,

00:29:05.570 --> 00:29:08.350
that my brain just loves that feeling

00:29:08.350 --> 00:29:10.620
and wants to then have
more accomplishments

00:29:10.620 --> 00:29:12.300
and check more things off.

00:29:12.300 --> 00:29:16.417
So that's my best advice, and
when I approach a new topic,

00:29:16.417 --> 00:29:18.297
I view it as an adventure, you know.

00:29:18.297 --> 00:29:20.600
And so it's just, you could
have approach life and this,

00:29:20.600 --> 00:29:22.350
this is like a mystery. it's like a game.

00:29:22.350 --> 00:29:25.490
And it's a puzzle and so
even if something looks dry

00:29:25.490 --> 00:29:28.220
or you're not sure how it
applies to your life at first,

00:29:28.220 --> 00:29:29.750
if you look at it with that lens,

00:29:29.750 --> 00:29:31.080
and if you look at it through the lens

00:29:31.080 --> 00:29:33.890
that you're able to
look at human knowledge

00:29:33.890 --> 00:29:36.640
that has been accumulated
over thousands of years

00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:39.730
and you are the beneficiary
of being able to consume that,

00:29:39.730 --> 00:29:42.410
you know, Isaac Newton
would have done anything

00:29:42.410 --> 00:29:44.890
to have access to the textbooks,

00:29:44.890 --> 00:29:46.960
the resources that we have today.

00:29:46.960 --> 00:29:48.590
Pythagoras would have done anything,

00:29:48.590 --> 00:29:50.290
Marie Curie would have done anything

00:29:50.290 --> 00:29:53.690
to have access to a modern
chemistry or biology book.

00:29:53.690 --> 00:29:57.510
We get that, and we get
the a lifetime's work

00:29:57.510 --> 00:30:00.230
of so many people who've
dedicated their lives to it.

00:30:00.230 --> 00:30:02.080
So if you view it that
way, it's quite exciting,

00:30:02.080 --> 00:30:05.080
I think to learn and it's fun
to meditate in a different way

00:30:05.080 --> 00:30:09.590
or it's fun to sit and
think about the learning

00:30:09.590 --> 00:30:12.560
in a way that forms those
neural connections in your brain

00:30:12.560 --> 00:30:13.620
and it becomes intuitive,

00:30:13.620 --> 00:30:15.440
and when you do that in one subject,

00:30:15.440 --> 00:30:17.090
all of a sudden a lot of other things

00:30:17.090 --> 00:30:18.880
start to make a lot of sense.

00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:21.130
So it looks like we're out of time.

